From 3deacc62e932876e075d4df24f2f403fc109f3a2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Scott Tromley <scotttromley@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 11:00:46 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] feat(layouts): per-column widths for the Scrolling layout

The Scrolling layout sizes columns as `work_area / min(columns,
container_count)`, so a strip holding fewer containers than `columns`
widens them to fill the work area. On a 32:9 panel a single container is
stretched across the full 7632px work area instead of occupying one
column, which is the opposite of how scrollable tiling behaves in PaperWM
and niri, where a column keeps its width and the rest of the strip is left
empty.

Resizing could not be used to work around this either. Three separate
places prevented a lone column from being resized:

- `calculate_scrolling_adjustment` returned early for `len <= 1`
- `enforce_resize_for_scrolling` called `enforce_no_resize` for 0 or 1
  containers, and always zeroed the last column's `right`
- `is_valid_direction` rejects Left for idx 0 and Right for the last
  container, so `resize-axis` has no valid direction with one container

Add two opt-in `ScrollingLayoutOptions`, both defaulting to unset so the
existing behaviour is unchanged when they are absent:

- `fixed_column_width` keeps columns at `work_area / columns` regardless
  of how many containers are present
- `width_steps` defines the width presets that the new
  `komorebic scrolling-cycle-column-width <next|previous>` snaps the
  focused column to, mirroring PaperWM's `cycle_width_steps` and
  `cycleWindowWidth`. Defaults to the golden ratio steps
  `[0.382, 0.5, 0.618]`

Unlike `column_ratios`, width steps are alternatives rather than a
partition of the work area, so `validate_width_steps` clamps, sorts and
deduplicates them without the cumulative sum rule that would otherwise
truncate `[0.382, 0.5, 0.618]` after the second entry.

Under `fixed_column_width` the strip is laid out from per-column widths
rather than a uniform grid, which changes two things:

- Widths are not run through `calculate_scrolling_adjustment`, whose
  neighbour compensation is right for a split layout but wrong for a
  strip. Widening a column pushes the columns to its right further along
  and leaves their widths alone. Both edges of the resize delta feed the
  width, so dragging either edge of a column with the mouse resizes that
  column and reflows the strip around it.
- The scroll position is a pixel offset carried forward from the previous
  layout rather than an index derived from a uniform column width, which
  is meaningless once columns differ in size. The strip scrolls forward
  far enough to bring the focused column's right edge on screen, then back
  far enough to bring its left edge on screen; in that order a column
  wider than the viewport ends up left-aligned, so the start of it is what
  you see.

Covered by 11 tests, including a regression test pinning the default
behaviour for a single container.

